---
/**
 * The dashboard's tier-1 recipe: the answer to "what should I do now?".
 *
 * Paper has no glass, no gradient and no panel-as-elevation (principles 1, 3 and 4),
 * so the tier that used to be a padded card with a gradient-filled action is re-derived
 * from four non-colour cues instead — position (first under the header), type size
 * (`text-title`, the largest text below the `<h1>`), text colour (full `--foreground`
 * against the notes' `--muted-foreground`) and control weight (the page's single filled
 * `Button`, passed in through the slot).
 *
 * Deliberately no top hairline: that absence is what says "this is not one of a list of
 * sections". The label/statement pair is the recipe ReviewSession.tsx:205-206 already
 * ships for a flashcard's front face, with sans substituted for serif — a dashboard
 * sentence is chrome reporting a count, not card content.
 *
 * The *statement* is the `<h2>`, not the eyebrow. Four of the five cues that make this
 * tier outrank a note are visual, so without this the hierarchy simply does not exist
 * for anyone not looking at the screen (WCAG 2.2 §1.3.1): heading navigation would
 * reach three chrome labels and never the one sentence that answers "what should I do
 * now?". The eyebrow stays a `<p>` that labels the section. Nothing moves visually —
 * both class lists are unchanged; only the elements carrying them differ. `DashboardNote`
 * drops to `<h3>` so it is subordinate in the outline exactly as it is on screen.
 *
 * Source: .ai/specs/2026-08-26-screen-migration-dashboard.md
 */
interface Props {
  label: string;
  statement: string;
}

const { label, statement } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="space-y-3">
  <p class="text-meta text-muted-foreground font-sans tracking-wide uppercase">{label}</p>
  <h2 class="text-foreground text-title font-sans">{statement}</h2>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3"><slot /></div>
</section>
